      <h1>{title}</h1>
      <p class="meta"><time datetime="2026-11-08">Published November 8, 2026</time></p>

      <p>Rakuten Kobo is testing a feature that would make it the first major e-reader maker to charge for something competitors give away: syncing your own files. The upcoming <strong>Send to Kobo</strong> system, currently rolling out through the Kobo 6.0+ beta firmware on the Kobo Libra Colour, Clara Colour, and Clara BW, requires a <strong>Kobo Stack</strong> subscription at <strong>C$3.99 per month</strong> (roughly US$2.90) to email or upload your personal EPUB and PDF collection to your e-reader.</p>

      <p>For EPUB power users — the people who sideload DRM-free books, convert formats, and manage libraries in tools like Calibre — this is a significant policy shift. Kobo has long been the refuge for readers who wanted an open alternative to Amazon's walled garden. This article breaks down exactly what Kobo Stack includes, how the pricing compares to the free alternatives, and what it means for your EPUB workflow.</p>

      <h2>What Is Kobo Stack?</h2>

      <p>Kobo Stack is a bundled subscription tier that wraps several services into one monthly fee. According to Kobo's beta documentation, the core deliverable is <strong>Send to Kobo</strong>, a cloud pipeline for getting personal content onto your device. The Stack bundle includes:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Email-to-device delivery.</strong> Every subscriber gets a unique Kobo Stack email address. Send an EPUB, PDF, or LCP-protected EPUB attachment to that address and the file appears in your Kobo library on any connected device, instantly.</li>
        <li><strong>Web upload tool.</strong> A drag-and-drop uploader in Kobo Cloud (on the main Kobo website) for batch-adding files without email.</li>
        <li><strong>Cross-device sync for sideloaded content.</strong> Reading positions, annotations, and highlights for your uploaded files sync across Kobo e-readers and the Kobo app — previously a perk reserved for Kobo Store purchases.</li>
        <li><strong>Libby multi-card support.</strong> Manage multiple library cards in the integrated OverDrive/Libby experience. Notably, the free tier already includes this.</li>
        <li><strong>Exclusive journaling workbooks.</strong> On the Libra Colour, subscribers get access to exclusive note-taking and journaling workbooks that leverage the color E Ink display.</li>
        <li><strong>Extended warranty.</strong> Three years of "worry-free reading" coverage replaces the default one-year manufacturer warranty.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How Send to Kobo Works in Practice</h2>

      <p>The workflow mirrors Amazon's Send to Kindle, which has been free since 2011. The difference is the paywall. Here's the step-by-step flow as described in the beta:</p>

      <ol>
        <li>Subscribe to Kobo Stack (C$3.99/month) and verify your unique Stack email address.</li>
        <li>Email an EPUB, PDF, or LCP EPUB as an attachment to your Stack address, or use the Kobo Cloud web uploader.</li>
        <li>The file lands in your Kobo library and syncs to every device signed into your account.</li>
        <li>Reading position, bookmarks, and annotations sync across devices — including Kobo's own apps for phone and desktop.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>Supported formats at launch are EPUB, PDF, and LCP EPUB (the DRM scheme used by many public libraries and DRM-free bookstores). Kobo's own KEPUB format and comic formats like CBZ are not part of the initial Send to Kobo pipeline.</p>

      <h2>Kobo Stack vs the Free Alternatives</h2>

      <p>The obvious question: is C$3.99/month worth it when every competitor offers the same core functionality for free? Here's the head-to-head:</p>

