        <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
          <h3 itemprop="name">What file formats does Kobo Stack accept?</h3>
          <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
            <div itemprop="text">
              <p>EPUB, PDF, and LCP-protected EPUB are supported at launch. Kobo's proprietary KEPUB format, comic formats like CBZ, and MOBI files are not accepted by the Send to Kobo pipeline. Files are delivered to your library as-is, without conversion.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
          <h3 itemprop="name">Is Amazon Send to Kindle still free?</h3>
          <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
            <div itemprop="text">
              <p>Yes. Amazon's Send to Kindle — via email, the web uploader at sendtokindle.com, or the mobile app — remains free with no subscription required. PocketBook and Onyx Boox also offer free cloud sync for personal files, making Kobo the first major e-reader maker to charge for this functionality.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
          <h3 itemprop="name">Can I still sideload EPUBs to my Kobo via USB for free?</h3>
          <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
            <div itemprop="text">
              <p>Yes. USB sideloading is unchanged and does not require a Kobo Stack subscription. You can transfer EPUB, PDF, KEPUB, and comic formats directly via USB cable using Calibre or simple file drag-and-drop. What you lose without Stack is cloud delivery, cross-device reading-position sync for sideloaded files, and annotation sync.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
